## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to translate a feature into a measurable business and engineering plan, focusing on CBA fundamentals, trade-offs, and governance for a client-side offline feature.

## Key Concepts

- **Client-side indexing and offline user experience**: Implementing lightweight search indexes (e.g., Lunr) for instant offline note search
- **Cost modeling**: Storage, CPU, latency, and privacy considerations
- **Rollout planning**: Phased deployment with validation metrics and maintenance scheduling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple Lunr index search
const index = lunr(function() {
  this.field('title', { boost: 10 });
  this.field('content');
  this.ref('id');
});

documents.forEach(doc => index.add(doc));

const results = index.search('search term');
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical CBA for a local-first search feature, balancing storage, CPU, and privacy.

## Key Concepts
- On-device indexing
- Delta sync costs
- Privacy/compliance
- Update cadence and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
function indexRepo(files) {
  // naive tokenization
  const index = {};
  for (const f of files) {
    const tokens = f.content.split(/\\W+/).filter(Boolean);
    tokens.forEach(t => (index[t] ||= []).push(f.path));
  }
  return index;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure user time saved and energy impact to validate the break-even?
- How would you design for monorepos and very large repos with incremental indexing?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates cost-awareness and decision-making capabilities when designing hybrid ML architectures with latency and privacy constraints—not just model accuracy.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid inference economics**: On-device versus edge cost trade-offs with intelligent data routing based on sensitivity, latency, and residency requirements.
- **Data residency and governance**: Regional compliance overhead and architecture implications.
- **Operational robustness**: Rollout strategy, drift detection, fault tolerance, and robust go/no-go criteria for production deployment.

## Code Example
```javascript
function npv(rate, flows) {
  let v = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < flows.length; i++) {
    v += flows[i] / Math.pow(1 + rate, i + 1);
  }
  return v;
}
```

This NPV function calculates net present value to evaluate whether the 18-month investment in the hybrid inference architecture yields positive returns and identifies the break-even horizon.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to quantify cross-cutting costs for a telemetry pipeline under real-world constraints (mobile energy, network variability, privacy).

## Key Concepts
- Cost-benefit modeling for streaming telemetry
- Adaptive sampling and batching trade-offs
- On-device resources vs backend costs
- Privacy, regulatory, and consent considerations
- Rollout strategy and break-even analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeCBA(params){
  const {events, batchSize, rate, energyPerEvent, dataCost} = params;
  const batches = Math.ceil(events / batchSize);
  const energy = batches * energyPerEvent * (rate/100);
  const data = events * dataCost * (rate/100);
  return {energy, data, batches};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would opt-in rate and drift affect the CBA over time?
- How would you validate and adjust thresholds in production?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, new angle on CBA: on-device prefetching to cut latency, balancing storage, energy, privacy, and business impact. Tests the ability to translate UX improvements into measurable costs and decision criteria.

## Key Concepts

- On-device predictor energy/compute
- Cache budgeting and invalidation strategies
- Data transfer vs. storage costs
- Privacy/opt-in and data minimization
- Latency impact and engagement metrics
- Break-even analysis and go/no-go thresholds

## Code Example

```javascript
function calcBreakeven(params){
  const {users, cacheMB, energyPerHit, savedMsPerHit, valuePerMs, pricePerMB} = params;
  const energyCost = energyPerHit * users;
  const dataValue = savedMsPerHit * users * valuePerMs;
  const breakevenMonths = (cacheMB * pricePerMB) / ((dataValue) - energyCost);
  return breakevenMonths;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you calibrate the model without A/B tests for small pilots?
- What privacy constraints would you enforce for on-device prefetching data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a lightweight edge optimization with clear cost/benefit trade-offs, focusing on data movement, energy, and privacy in mobile environments.

## Key Concepts

- Content-based fingerprinting
- Bloom filters for on-device dedup
- Bandwidth vs CPU energy trade-offs
- Privacy considerations and optional cloud verification

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: compute hash and check Bloom filter
function canSend(record, bloom) {
  const h = hash(record); // deterministic fingerprint
  if (bloom.mightContain(h)) return false;
  bloom.add(h);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

